// The reference table of styles and vibes as the genre research of 2026-11-27 wrote it (§12 of the
// report filed as `research_notes/2026-11-27_genres-and-vibes.md` in the private repo
// echoelmusic-intern): one JSON object per line, 68 styles (33 electronic, 25 band and acoustic,
// 10 traditions) and 13 vibes, each with its sources and how certain it is. `StyleLibrary` decodes
// and checks it once, on first use; `StyleLibraryTests` holds that it reads whole and says nothing
// the language law forbids.
//
// Edit the table here, as data, one line per entry. Some fields are the research's picture of
// Studio on that day and are never read as the truth: `scales_missing_in_studio`,
// `tunings_missing_in_studio` and `modes_missing_in_studio` (a name resolves against the build that
// runs), `drums.studio_kit` (what Studio's kit plays is the kit's to say). `studio_amount` and
// `beyond_studio` are checked against the swing formula and never used in its place.
//
// Changes to the research's data, each for the text meant for the screen (the report's §2):
// - funk: "clavinet-like keys", a maker's trademark, reads "electric clavichord";
// - two vibes take musical words before the assistant names any id, so no screen borrows the
//   framing of sleep or hypnosis: `dreamy` ("Dreamy", "hazy and floating") is `hazy` ("Hazy",
//   "soft, blurred and floating"), and `hypnotic` ("Hypnotic", "repetitive, steady and slowly
//   changing") is `repetitive` ("Repetitive", "steady, looping and slowly changing"); their
//   `uncertainty.notes` keep the research's words;
// - ethiopian: the "(UNVERIFIED)" that ended `felt` stands at the end of `uncertainty.notes`, so a
//   field for the screen carries no research marker.

enum StyleLibraryData {
    static let json = #"""
